WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:03.159
Sneakers.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:04.899
The Rubber Soul innovation of the

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.089
late 1800s became pop culture

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.919
staples by the 1970s

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.899
and 80s, with models like

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:11.900
the Adidas Superstar, PUMA Clyde,

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:16.209
and Nike Air Force 1.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:17.829
But it wasn't until the release of

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:19.472
the Air Jordan in 1985

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.389
that sneaker fandom became an

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.159
international obsession.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.279
Here at Peep My Kickz sneaker

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:28.749
convention in New Brunswick, New

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:30.699
Jersey, resellers feed

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.679
Sneakerheads' voracious appetite

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.629
for Nike's, especially

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:35.799
as Air Jordan line.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.580
This is pair of Off White Jordan 1

00:00:37.581 --> 00:00:38.380
Highs. I'm trying to actually sell

00:00:38.381 --> 00:00:38.829
it.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:40.869
Resellers act like speculative

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.269
investors stocking up on inventory

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.279
as they bet that prices will rise,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.139
sometimes yielding enormous

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:47.799
profits.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:48.800
That's 220?

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.309
Appreciate you, bro. You got
Instagram?

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.169
But there is one person here who is

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:55.149
not selling Jordans, at least not

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:56.799
as Nike conceived of this shoe.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.779
He goes by the name Naady and sells

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.519
a reimagined version of an Air

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.739
Jordan 1 under his own independent

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.899
clothing label "Global Heartbreak".

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.369
We on our third shoe.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:07.779
He is part of a controversial

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.669
segment of the sneakers market.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:11.529
Small label designers who

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.359
make products derivative of Air

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.759
Jordans and other Nike models.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.409
It's all just trying to push our

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:18.219
artwork. Trying to push the next

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.449
man. I got a platform.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:20.709
Why not use it?

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.389
Now he runs his label out of his

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.129
apartment in North Brunswick, New

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:25.130
Jersey.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.179
This is my closet.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.919
Right now this is probably the only

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.149
OGs I have.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:32.679
I got him restored like twice

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.389
because they start to yellow, they

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:35.799
start to crack.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.120
Black Air Force energy.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.199
Yeah, this is only 50 made. I
probably

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.329
spent the most money on these,

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:44.379
and this might have been a bad

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.299
purchase. Covid happened and

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.499
school kicked us out.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.419
We had 60%

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:51.279
of our dorming back and I was

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.709
like "Oh yeah, let me just

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:55.179
make a dumb decision".

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.010
And these were like 1800.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.289
Naady began Global Heartbreak with

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:04.539
only $50 to his name,

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.249
steadily building a following

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:08.409
through Instagram over the past six

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:09.459
years.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.919
This is supposed to be the needle

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.139
and this is the broken heart, and

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.579
the situation I was going through,

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:16.629
which is the heartbreak.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.789
And then the needle would be

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:20.619
clothing. But then it takes two

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:21.745
people to mend a broken heart, and

00:02:21.746 --> 00:02:24.399
sew it back up together.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:25.989
Naady works on sneaker designs with

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:27.999
his childhood friend who goes by

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:28.779
Teeb.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:30.699
My art is best when I feed off other

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.779
people's ideas and

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.269
Naady is just somebody that we just

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:35.479
clicked.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.519
Remember when I had the little

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:36.549
carrot?

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:37.227
The carrot?

00:02:37.228 --> 00:02:38.559
Yeah. Why you take that off?

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.269
Because it was ugly.

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:42.369
In today's decentralized makers

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.439
economy, despite running a small

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.629
independent clothing label,

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:48.309
Naady was able to contract directly

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:50.289
with a manufacturer in China to

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:51.969
produce his inventory.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:53.799
I'd send them the file of exactly

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.869
what I want. I ask the manufacturers

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.239
to send videos of the whole process

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.240
from scratch.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:01.629
You could get a good sneaker for

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:03.579
about $65.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.789
We sell 100 shoes at $150.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:08.080
We didn't even triple profit.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.409
I don't like to break pockets.

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:12.609
I want the people from where I'm

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:14.049
from to be able to purchase it.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:15.819
We made money back, but it was more

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.070
so about pushing our art.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.649
Although Naady and Teeb don't

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:22.389
produce enough shoes for Nike to

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.399
take notice, the sneaker giant

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.349
has been cracking down on derivative

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:27.249
designers.

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:29.259
The most high profile lawsuit is

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.756
against trendsetting fashion brand

00:03:30.757 --> 00:03:32.679
"A bathing ape" or

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.239
BAPE for short.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.489
Bape is a really important Japanese

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:37.719
streetwear brand that started in the

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.549
1990s. It was embraced by

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:41.229
hip hop superstars Jay-Z and

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:42.639
Pharrell, probably the biggest among

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:43.089
them.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:45.009
Brendan Dunne is the editor

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:46.629
of the leading online sneaker

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:48.459
magazine Sole Collector, and

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:50.349
co-hosts the YouTube sneaker

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.509
show Full Size Run.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:54.579
BAPE was most known in sneakers for

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.259
making the BAPE STA, which is a

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:57.759
version of the Air Force 1 that

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.679
replaces the Nike swoosh with

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.419
a shooting star logo across the

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:03.219
side. If you look at the shoe and

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:04.599
you know anything about sneakers,

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.319
you know immediately that's based on

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:07.179
Air Force 1.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.979
I wanted BAPE STA bad when I was a

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:10.539
teenager, and I'm happy to own a few

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:11.359
pairs now.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:12.429
You know, you put this next to a

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:13.959
Nike Air Force 1 and it should be

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.078
pretty obvious the DNA of the shoes.

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:17.169
You don't have to be a sneaker

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:18.518
obsessive to see the things that

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:20.049
these models have in common.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:21.009
This one came first.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:22.010
This one came later. This

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:25.959
year, decades after the

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:27.879
original release of the BAPE STA,

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:29.829
Nike finally sued BAPE over

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:31.809
the design, calling the

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.309
BAPE STA a "knock off Air Force 1",

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:35.289
saying that BAPE was infringing

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:37.149
upon Nike's intellectual

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:38.799
property, the trade dress around the

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.959
Air Force 1, and asking

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:42.789
the court to stop BAPE

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:44.169
from selling these shoes.

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:45.999
And this action from Nike comes

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.069
amid a wave of recent litigation

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:49.299
where they're trying to swat down

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.249
all these makers who create these

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:53.109
shoes that look a lot like Nike

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:54.939
shoes and are very much based

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:57.069
on the success of Nike shoes.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:59.259
It's hard to know why exactly

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:00.939
Nike has been so aggressive

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:02.379
recently. I would speculate that

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:03.879
there are just more of them now than

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:04.929
there ever were before.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:06.699
And this looks to Nike like a whole

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:08.169
cottage industry that could get out

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:09.039
of control.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:10.509
It seems like every six months

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.489
there's a new version of the Jordan

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:15.029
1 with a different logo on it.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.439
There's some people that are just

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.029
like, "Oh, those are knock offs.

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:19.399
Those are for Fugazi", but then

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:20.849
there's some people that really look

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.009
at it as "Alright, this is

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:24.659
limited, a collectible".

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:26.099
Like especially when you go hard

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.749
with the box and then you separate

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.999
yourself with the colorway.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.439
I feel like, from an artistic

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.539
standpoint, like everything

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:34.649
comes from something.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.249
Nothing's truly original.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:38.999
Like, yeah, this is something you've

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.829
seen before, but it doesn't

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:42.689
take anything away from being

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:43.690
personal to us.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.779
We usually think that copying

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:50.429
actually destroys incentives to

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.349
innovate, but in the fashion world,

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.059
copying is actually what creates the

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.859
incentives to innovate.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:58.409
NYU Law professor Christopher

00:05:58.410 --> 00:05:59.699
Sprigman is the coauthor of "The

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.339
Knockoff Economy: How Imitation

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:03.569
Sparks Innovation".

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:05.099
In the book, he draws a sharp

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.109
distinction between copyright,

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:09.569
trademark and trade dress.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.219
So copyright protects artistic and

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:12.269
literary works.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:14.849
Trademark is about symbols,

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:16.649
words, sounds

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.129
that we use to identify the source

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:21.029
of products.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:22.859
Trade dress has a bit of a different

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.229
flavor to it, which is sometimes

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.449
the shape of products or elements

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:29.369
of a product's construction tell us

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:30.689
where the product comes from.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.549
Trade dress is enforced

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:33.449
in the fashion industry

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.129
occasionally, but what you get in a

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:36.329
lot of lawsuits is some pretty

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:39.119
dubious claims of design

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:40.649
being recognizable.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.179
When these lawsuits happen,

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.699
inevitably, the Nike litigators

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:46.229
will come up with screenshots from

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:48.209
Instagram comments of people who are

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:49.709
confused and are thinking this is a

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:51.179
Nike shoe on some level.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:52.709
But I believe those people are in

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:54.569
the extreme, extreme minority and

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:56.159
anybody really spending their money

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:58.289
on shoes like this knows that Nike

00:06:58.290 --> 00:06:59.639
didn't produce them or Nike is not

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:00.449
involved.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:02.339
Copying helps set trends.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:03.869
Trends sell fashion.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:06.299
Copying is what helps kill trends.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:07.799
Copying is what helps set the trend

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:08.819
that comes next.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:10.709
So the fashion cycle runs,

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:11.999
and the fashion industry's

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.589
successive waves of innovation

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:15.480
depend on copying.

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:19.139
But Sprigman sees a big difference

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:21.299
between derivatives, such as

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:22.859
BAPE's or Naady's designs and

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.839
replicas, or reps for

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:27.089
short, which attempt to mimic Nike's

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.099
models exactly making

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:31.169
them a clear case of trademark

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:32.309
infringement.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:34.199
Replica sneakers is not something

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:35.570
that I personally am a fan of.

00:07:36.658 --> 00:07:37.469
It's totally fake.

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:38.519
It's what we call in the street,

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:38.521
"fu-fu".

00:07:38.522 --> 00:07:40.319
We don't do replicas.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:41.519
Replicas are not it.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.139
I was always taught, you can't

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:44.549
afford it? Don't wear it.

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.649
I would never ever wear a replica

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:47.939
sneaker. But when you think about

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.619
fake sneakers from the era when I

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:51.209
started collecting, they were more

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:52.859
obviously fake.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:54.779
A lot of the money spent these

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:56.489
days on replica sneakers has to do

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:58.319
with recreations of shoes that

00:07:58.320 --> 00:07:59.969
are not authorized by the companies,

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:01.919
but still look exactly like

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:03.779
and feel exactly like or as close

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:05.459
to exact as possible.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:06.839
I think it's definitely ironic if

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:08.069
you consider the fact that some of

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:09.389
these shoes, the fake ones versus

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:10.799
the real ones, were made in the same

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.839
factories by the same labor force.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:14.699
That is one of the harder pieces to

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:16.649
really square away in terms of not

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:18.269
wanting to everywhere fake shoes.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:19.799
So if we look at the placement of

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:21.419
the Nike Air on the label, you can

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:23.039
see that the air logo at the bottom

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:24.629
is placed slightly higher and the

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:26.429
label is actually a lot smaller and

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.259
tighter on the fake compared

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:29.609
to the retail one.

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:31.619
Videos like this help buyers

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:33.569
distinguish between replicas and

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:34.739
the real thing.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.658
But most sneakerheads aren't fooled

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:38.158
by replicas.

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:40.168
They buy them knowingly because

00:08:40.169 --> 00:08:42.209
the differences are so subtle

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:44.429
and the real thing is out of reach.

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:46.499
So Nike and Adidas in a sense,

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.209
have learned what the luxury goods

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:49.499
game is about, and they're getting

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:50.729
into that game.

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:53.069
They purposely manufacture

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:55.319
fewer than could be sold.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:56.879
They create scarcity.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.979
The scarcity helps create a mania.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:00.779
This mania helps drive up the price

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:02.309
on secondary markets.

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:03.569
It seems to me that that kind of

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:05.189
behavior is going to call forth

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:07.169
counterfeits the way rain calls for

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:07.949
flowers.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.569
That's just the way the world works.

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:11.579
Unlike with knockoffs,

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:12.629
Sprigman believes that companies

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:14.069
have a legitimate case against

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:15.959
replicas, but also sees it as

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:17.639
a form of class bias.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:18.839
People should understand what

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:19.769
they're getting.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:21.329
But if a person buys a counterfeit

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:23.189
and is not fooled, I guess

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:24.239
the problem is that you'll be

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.129
walking out in the world with a very

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:27.899
high quality, counterfeit luxury

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:29.819
good that other people will mistake

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:30.989
for the real thing.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:32.189
When they mistake it for the real

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:33.629
thing, they will impute to you the

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:35.429
status that in a sense, you don't

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:37.589
have. We have to ask ourselves,

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:38.789
are we really in the business of

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:41.849
regulating people's status?

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:43.709
That's a business that says that the

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:46.259
person without a lot of money cannot

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:48.689
essentially signal to the world

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:50.159
that they have the kind of taste or

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:51.719
that they have the kind of means

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:53.429
that a person with more money has.

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:55.469
This is a way of segmenting

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:57.479
us by economic class, using

00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:59.249
intellectual property law as the

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:01.409
means. And that is a very

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:03.119
normatively unattractive thing for

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:04.229
intellectual property law to be

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:04.889
doing.

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:06.869
I do think Nike is doing fine, but

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:08.339
I do still think it's in their best

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:10.439
interest to combat counterfeits

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:11.999
and knockoffs, because I believe

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:13.739
that does divert revenue away from

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:14.569
them.

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:15.949
But I feel like Nike's battle

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:17.779
against bootleg makers is a game

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:19.129
of whack a mole more than anything

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:20.130
else.

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:25.499
BAPE is still selling its sneakers

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:26.969
and the company has filed for

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:29.219
dismissal of Nike's lawsuit.

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:30.959
A handful of other cases have been

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:32.879
dropped or settled out of court,

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:34.799
with derivative designers agreeing

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.049
to stop selling their products.

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:39.089
It's still not clear if Nike's legal

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:41.039
campaign will succeed in shutting

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:42.809
down this segment of the sneakers

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:43.709
market.

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:45.599
I see myself expanding.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.579
I think kids now are

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:49.229
open to wearing different shoes

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:51.419
outside of just Nike Air Jordans.

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:53.489
There there's so many creators

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:54.599
because I feel like social media

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:55.600
plays a big part.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:58.709
Fashion creativity comes from

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:00.269
designers looking at what other

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:02.069
designers are doing and playing with

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:03.029
those ideas.

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:04.499
Copying is part of the cycle that

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:05.519
drives the industry forward.

